{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_c2rldelxbeh7nx5gqw4oeqicpd2lhbsh562ddejgtyn2r55ckefa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01LhmkyU1tPqykp5voVn7VoJ","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Audit C vs Rust line by line","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"You are auditing a C-to-Rust translation for EXACT behavioural equivalence. Do NOT modify any files. Produce a report only.\n\nWorking directory: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-T82EWy/translated_rust\n\n- C ground truth: c_src/src/lib.c (956 lines, an stb_ds.h-derived dynamic array + hash map, plus `strkey` and `arr_push` helpers at the bottom)\n- Rust translation: src/lib.rs (1181 lines)\n\nThe C is ALWAYS correct. Your job is to find places where the Rust could behave DIFFERENTLY from the C for some input. Go function by function through EVERY exported function and every static helper:\n\nstbds_arrgrowf, stbds_arrfreef, stbds_rand_seed, stbds_hash_string, stbds_siphash_bytes/stbds_hash_bytes, stbds_probe_position, stbds_log2, stbds_make_hash_index (incl. the stbds_load_32_or_64 macro), stbds_is_key_equal, stbds_hmfree_func, stbds_hm_find_slot, stbds_hmget_key_ts, stbds_hmget_key, stbds_hmput_default, stbds_strdup, stbds_hmput_key, stbds_shmode_func, stbds_hmdel_key, stbds_stralloc, stbds_strreset, strkey, arr_push.\n\nFor each, check with extreme care:\n1. Integer type widths and signedness at EVERY operation (C integer promotion of `unsigned char`/`char` to `int`, `int`->`size_t` sign extension, `ptrdiff_t` vs `size_t` mixing, shifts that overflow `int`).\n2. Order of side effects (which field is written first; `x = y = expr` chains; comma operators; pre/post increment).\n3. Pointer arithmetic: does the Rust scale the same way as the C? (e.g. `t+1` on a `stbds_hash_index*` advances 104 bytes; `(char*)a + elemsize*i`).\n4. Branch conditions: `>=` vs `>` vs `==`, and the exact constants.\n5. Struct field layout and offsets.\n6. Loop bounds and fall-through (the `switch (len - i)` in siphash falls through cases 7..1).\n7. Anything the C does that the Rust \"cleaned up\" or vice versa.\n8. Any place the Rust uses NON-wrapping arithmetic (`+`, `-`, `*`, `<<`) where the C value could overflow/wrap — in a debug build that PANICS whereas C wraps. List every such site with the input that would trigger it, or state that it is unreachable and why.\n9. Any place the Rust uses `wrapping_shl`/`wrapping_shr` where C would have well-defined (non-UB) behaviour that DIFFERS from Rust's masking semantics.\n\nReport findings as a table with: function | C line(s) | Rust line(s) | concern | severity (DIVERGENCE / RISKY / OK-but-note) | the concrete input that would expose it.\n\nBe exhaustive and concrete. Cite actual line numbers and quote the actual code. If you find NO divergence in a function, say so explicitly in one line. Do not speculate vaguely — either give an input that exposes a difference, or explain why none exists."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10597,"cache_read_input_tokens":314680,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10597,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"81ea8718-705d-4eca-b08c-039f28f83148","uuid":"7718d053-2162-414d-867e-d4f3b06aedc4","timestamp":"2026-08-20T07:11:35.169Z"}
